{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"reasoning","text":"scipy is available. Implement template matching:\n\n1. Protein point cloud: 2D points (x, y) from planar projection (2388 points).\n2. For each candidate character (B,D,F,H,J,L,N,P,R,T,V,X,Z): rasterize the glyph at about 200x200 using matplotlib's Agg (draw text, save to buffer, read pixels), extract ink pixels as points.\n3. Normalize both: center by centroid (or bbox center), scale so that the max bbox dimension = 1 (preserve aspect ratio).\n4. For 8 transformations (rotations 0/90/180/270 × mirror), compute symmetric chamfer distance using scipy.spatial.cKDTree.\n5. Report distances; pick the minimum.\n\nNote: glyph aspect ratio: characters are usually taller than wide. The protein's bbox is 304 x 131 in PC1-PC2 (aspect ratio 2.3). Characters like K have aspect ratio ~0.7-0.8. If we only allow uniform scaling, the aspect mismatch will be penalized, but that's fine — matching should prefer characters with similar aspect ratio under rotation: rotating the protein 90° gives aspect ratio 0.43 (taller than wide) — characters have aspect ratio ~0.7. Hmm, the protein is very elongated (2.3:1). Characters are ~0.7-0.8 (H is ~0.9, X ~0.8, T ~0.8, V ~0.8, Z ~0.8, N ~0.8, L ~0.7, J ~0.6, F ~0.6, B ~0.7, D ~0.75, P ~0.7, R ~0.75). Rotated protein aspect ratio = 131/304 = 0.43. Hmm, none match well; but chamfer will pick the best relative one.\n\nWait, but should we allow non-uniform scaling (stretching)? The designed protein might be stretched. If we allow independent x/y scaling to the unit square, aspect ratio is ignored and topology dominates. That might be better for character identification. Allow both uniform and per-axis scaling? Simpler: normalize each to a unit bbox square (per-axis scaling), test 8 transformations, and use chamfer. The character with the best topology wins.\n\nAlso, to keep it fair, sample the glyph ink uniformly (all ink pixels downsampled to ~3000 points), and use ~3000 protein points.\n\nChamfer: mean of NN distances in both directions, in unit-square coordinates.\n\nLet's write the code."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\chenyuqiong\\\\.cache\\\\codex-runtimes\\\\codex-primary-runtime\\\\dependencies\\\\native\\\\powershell\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"@'\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use('Agg')\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom io import BytesIO\nfrom scipy.spatial import cKDTree\nimport matplotlib.image as mpimg\n\nX = np.load('ca_coords.npy')\nXc = X - X.mean(0)\nU, S, Vt = np.linalg.svd(Xc, full_matrices=False)\nP = Xc @ Vt.T\npts = P[:, :2].copy()\n\ndef norm_unit(a):\n    a = a - a.mean(0)\n    mn, mx = a.min(0), a.max(0)\n    return (a - mn) / (mx - mn)   # per-axis to [0,1]\n\ndef glyph_points(ch, n=4000):\n    fig = plt.figure(figsize=(3,3), dpi=100)\n    ax = fig.add_axes([0,0,1,1]); ax.axis('off')\n    ax.text(0.5, 0.5, ch, fontsize=200, ha='center', va='center', family='DejaVu Sans')\n    buf = BytesIO(); fig.savefig(buf, format='png', dpi=100); plt.close(fig)\n    buf.seek(0); img = mpimg.imread(buf)\n    alpha = img[..., 3] if img.shape[2] == 4 else (1 - img[..., :3].mean(-1))\n    ys, xs = np.where(alpha > 0.5)\n    p = np.stack([xs, -ys], 1).astype(float)\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(0)\n    if len(p) > n: p = p[rng.choice(len(p), n, replace=False)]\n    return p\n\ndef transforms(p):\n    out = []\n    q = p.copy()\n    for _ in range(4):\n        out.append(q.copy())\n        out.append(np.stack([-q[:,0], q[:,1]], 1))\n        q = np.stack([q[:,1], q[:,0]], 1)  # 90 deg rotation\n    return out\n\ndef chamfer(a, b):\n    ta, tb = cKDTree(a), cKDTree(b)\n    da, _ = ta.query(b, k=1)\n    db, _ = tb.query(a, k=1)\n    return 0.5 * (da.mean() + db.mean())\n\nprot = norm_unit(pts)\nresults = {}\nfor ch in list('BDFHJLNPRTVXZ'):\n    g0 = norm_unit(glyph_points(ch))\n    best = min(chamfer(prot, t) for t in transforms(g0))\n    results[ch] = best\n    print(ch, round(best, 4))\norder = sorted(results, key=results.get)\nprint('RANKING:', [(c, round(results[c],4)) for c in order])\n'@ | Set-Content -Encoding utf8 match_letters.py; python match_letters.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
